   The tutorial recently made another appearance on the net, and Ken Arromdee
(Mr. Source Code) posted a number of corrections to it. Some of the things
*may* only apply to 2.3, but I think they're all valid for 2.2. Anyway,
I figured you might want this so you can edit the tutorial yourself.

Fixes to part one:
-------------------------------------
>.... You can throw anything, but
>it is strongly recommended that you use a weapon if you want to do damage to
>the creature.

Note there are exceptions: a heavy iron ball, enormous rock, cream pie,
potion of sickness, etc....

>the letter of the object you wish to wield. Be careful that you are not 
>bashing a creature with your bow, as this is not very effective. The same 
>applies for slings and rocks. [And crossbows and bolts. -PG]

Warning: "slings and rocks" is wrong.  Sling bullets are thrown out of
slings.  Rocks are not.

>E [?*- or a letter] <phrase>
>    Once the writing implement is selected, the user is prompted for a
>  phrase. This phrase is written in that spot. If a sharp object or magic
>  marker was used, the engraving is permanent...

Make that _almost_ permanent.  It does eventually wear out.

>             To dip an item into a pool, stand over the pool and use the dip 
>             command.

You may dip into a fountain.  You may not dip into a pool, and unless levitating
will drown if you try to walk into one.

---------------------------------
Fixes to part two:
---------------------------------

>dead lizard          3, 1,   40 Try when turning to stone.

Also cures confusion after a 1 turn wait.

>carrot               2, 1,   50 May improve vision

Cures blindness in Nethack, does nothing unusual in Hack.

>NOTE:  It is advised that the user not save meat for long periods of time,
>      as it may become tainted. ...

Exception: acid blobs do not become tainted.

>dead stalker          0,  40,   400   Gives you see invisible.
>                                      (Also invisibility if invisible?)

Early Hack versions: invisibility and confusion.
Later Hack, and Nethack: if you're visible, it gives you invisibility
	and confusion.  If you're invisible, it gives you confusion as
	well as _permanent_ invisibility and _permanent_ see invisible.

>dead yellow light     0,    1,   10   Confuses, May improve vision

Does not improve vision.

>enchant weapon  6

Do not try on a weapon at > +6, or it may evaporate.

>wishing                 1   Actually this chance is even smaller.

Says who? :-)

>taming - Any creature next to the user becomes the user's pet.

Exception: ghosts, long worms, shopkeepers, other humans, the Wizard of Yendor.

>magic missile,fire,cold,death,sleep - (D) Fires a bolt of that type in the
>        direction indicated. Death will kill any creature it hits,
>        while sleep will sleep any creature it hits.

Death will not kill undead (Z, W, V, ' ') or demons.

>warning - The ring will glow when a monster is generated.

It glows when a monster is nearby (not when it's generated) with a timeout
between glowings.

>confuse monster - Normal effect.

No.  Your hands glow purple and it confuses _you_ instead of the monster.

>blank paper - Normal effect.

No.  "You see strange patterns on this scroll."

>gold detection - see fake gold

This "fake gold" is actually the locations of all the real traps (though the
gold symbol gets used).

>food detection - see fake food

This "fake food" is actually real potions (though the food symbol is used).
Pre-Nethack versions had a display bug when detecting potions this way.

>punishment - ?

"You feel guilty." And you're not punished.

>#dip
>    Dip arrows or darts in the potion and if it was a potion of:
>    Sickness or Paralysis --> your dipped weapon gets stronger.
>    Holy water --> your weapon gets blessed and is better in use against the
>                   undead.

Shurikens and crossbow bolts may also be dipped in sickness.
ALL weapons can be blessed from holy water.
Any item (not just weapons) can be decursed from holy water.

>#sit
>   Use this command while you are on an throne (\).  You might get a wish
>   (Chance 1 in 86), but also you can lose your gold or your possessions get
>   cursed or identified!! [I don't know what the author meant by your
>   possessions getting identified. This sounds like a good thing to me. -PG]

Like an identify scroll.

[Okay, that's what it sounded like. The context confused me. -PG]

>    First, the gem must be an actual gem, and not a piece of colored glass. 
>  Also, if the gem has been identified as being real, then the increase in
>  the user's luck will be much greater than if the gem the was unidentified. 

If unidentified, any gem including glass is accepted and gains an increase of 1.
If identified, valuable gems gain 5, and glass isn't accepted.

>  Another, more more useful method, is to get a teleport control ring and
>  teleport next to the unicorn (or along a line of sight). The unicorn does
>  not have a chance to move away, since teleporting does not count as a turn
>  for movement purposes.

It does count as a turn.  However, the timing is such that an
at-random-intervals teleport comes after monster movement, so such a teleport
will work.  A teleport from reading a scroll or using CTRL-T won't work
(unless you're hasted).

>   When a dog becomes stranded, he becomes wild.

Only if you wait too long before returning.

>   A pet has other abilities as well. The first is that the pet will tend
>not to walk over cursed items.

Unless there's food on top of the cursed item and the dog intends to eat it.

>.... The nice thing about ghost levels is that all of the old character's
>items are in one pile on the space the character died. It is a simple matter
>to get these items.

However, many of them will be cursed.

